# {{.Name}}

A wefty workflow scaffolded by `wefty workflow init {{.Name}}`. It greets a
subject, reports one step, one envelope and one gate, and hands back a result
document. Replace the work; keep the reporting shape.

## What this workflow may do

It reports, and it uses no credential to do it. Reporting goes through the
**run mailbox** — files written into `$WEFTY_RUN_DIR`, which the node agent
publishes to the run ledger with the credential it already holds. A mailbox
write is a claim about this run and nothing else: it confers no authority over
the cluster, so do not build on this workflow being able to dispatch a child
run, read another run, or act on any other job.

The protocol is `docs/contracts/run-execution-context.md`, "Run mailbox". The
mailbox is delivered to process one-shots that L3 dispatched; an OCI attempt
does not receive one yet.

## Files

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `{{.Name}}.sh` | the workflow |
| `{{.TestFile}}` | `bash -n`, shellcheck, and a local run that asserts the four events |

`{{.Name}}.sh` calls `wefty run ...` when the `wefty` binary is on PATH and
falls back to an inline POSIX writer otherwise. The two produce byte-identical
event files, which is what will let an image that does not ship the binary
report the same way once OCI attempts receive a mailbox.

The fallback's parameter reader is deliberately limited — top-level string
params, no JSON escapes — so prefer `wefty run params NAME` when the binary is
there. The exact limits are documented above `wefty_param` in the script.

## Run it

Submit it to the cluster, follow it, then read what it reported:

```sh
wefty --json submit \
  --script={{.ScriptName}} \
  --interpreter=bash \
  --params-file=params.json \
  --tag=<routing-tag> \
  --required-envelope \
  --idempotency-key=<stable-unique-key>

wefty logs <run_id> --follow      # wait for it, live
wefty --json inspect <run_id>     # the envelopes, gates and lineage it produced
```

`--required-envelope` is not optional advice: without it a job that exits 0
having reported nothing still looks like a success, which is the one failure
this whole reporting shape exists to prevent.

`params.json` is an ordinary JSON object; this starter reads `subject`:

```json
{"subject": "wefty"}
```

A job never receives its own params in the environment. The agent delivers them
to `$WEFTY_RUN_DIR/params.json`, which is what `wefty run params subject` reads.

## Local exercise

The workflow does not need a cluster to be exercised: point `WEFTY_RUN_DIR` at
an empty directory and run it, then read `events/`.

```sh
export WEFTY_RUN_DIR=$(mktemp -d)
export WEFTY_HANDOFF_DIR=$(mktemp -d)
bash {{.Name}}.sh
ls "$WEFTY_RUN_DIR/events"
```

`{{.TestFile}}` does exactly that. The full single-machine stack exercise —
submit, dispatch, publish, inspect — is modelled in
`workflows/branch-gates/branchgates_integration_test.go`.
